WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.040
"Pesticides (DDT) and
Alzheimer’s Disease"

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.440
Although there is a growing list
of Alzheimer's disease

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.400
susceptibility genes, even if
you put them all altogether,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.760
they account for less than half
of all Alzheimer's cases.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.880
The single most compelling piece
of data on the potential control

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.360
we have over the disease
is the fact that if you have

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.800
identical twins, with the exact
same genes, even if one gets

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.760
Alzheimer's the other
usually does not.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.360
So, we have to think
about all the other

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.200
contributing factors
beyond just genetics.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.800
In my video on pesticides and cancer,
I talked about this study.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.160
There’s a list of chlorinated
pesticides, including DDE

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.000
(a metabolite of DDT) that
are classified by the EPA

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.240
as probable human carcinogens.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.200
But in the study, blood
levels of DDE and others were

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.240
associated not with
increased cancer mortality,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.360
but increased risk of
other-cause mortality.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.680
This led researchers to
speculate it may be due to

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.000
an associated increased risk
of diabetes or dementia.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.640
I’ve talked previously
about the diabetes link.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.800
What about dementia?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.920
“Elevated Serum Pesticide Levels
and Risk for Alzheimer Disease.”

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.280
A research team at Rutgers found
significantly higher blood

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.760
levels of DDE in Alzheimer’s disease
patients compared to controls,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.520
and autopsy studies
show blood levels

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.400
are a good proxy for brain levels.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.640
Those with the highest levels
were at about four times

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.960
the odds of being
demented with Alzheimer's.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.880
And in a petri dish, DDE increases
amyloid precursor protein levels

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.040
in human brain cells, providing
a potential mechanism.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.920
Here are the levels of the
sticky protein implicated in the

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.520
development of Alzheimer's
disease before and after some DDE

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.360
is added, at the levels one finds
circulating in highly exposed

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.600
individuals in the general population.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.320
Put all these studies together,
and there does indeed seem

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.360
to be a link, consistent with
data showing about a doubling

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.880
of risk for developing dementia among

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.760
those acutely pesticide poisoned.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.680
Among U.S. elders, DDT and its
breakdown product DDE are also

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.680
associated with increased risk
of cognitive decline in general.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.320
DDT was extensively
used in the United States

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.240
from the 1940s through the 1960s.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.480
At its peak, we were churning
out 180 million pounds a year.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.120
And it’s still in our bodies to this day,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.640
contaminating the bloodstreams of
more than 90 percent of Americans,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.520
and DDE, the pesticide
linked to quadrupling the odds

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.240
of Alzheimer’s, were at the highest levels.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.520
It’s still in our bodies because
it’s still in the food supply.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.320
In my last video on the topic,
I noted that the levels of

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.440
DDT, DDE, and other banned
pesticides and pollutants

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.640
were much lower in the breast
milk from a vegetarian mother

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.840
compared to breast milk of
her non-vegetarian sister.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.400
And the largest difference
was noted for DDE,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.920
which was four times lower
in the vegetarian sister.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.920
This is what you see across the
board for these kinds of pollutants.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.200
Food samples were collected from
supermarkets across the U.S.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.360
Here’s what they found
for dioxins and PCBs

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.920
in beef, chicken, pork,
processed meat, eggs, fish,

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.040
all plant-foods put together,
and dairy products.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.080
These toxins build up the food
chain; so, it makes sense that

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.560
the most contaminated foods are
meat, fish, and dairy products.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.160
5 to 10 times higher
levels in meat, eggs,

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.760
fish, and dairy than what
they found in plant foods.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.440
And unfortunately, cooking doesn’t
destroy pollutants like DDE—

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.040
in fact it may make them
even more concentrated.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.600
And this is for a pesticide
that may increase the risk

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.021
of Alzheimer’s disease as
much as if you carried

00:03:40.021 --> 00:03:43.499
the so-called Alzheimer’s
gene APOE e4.

